+# Committed speaker projection — 7 October 2026
+
+Status: the second optimized candidate passes all **51** isolated actual producer/consumer checks through `--selftest-meeting-context`. The same fourteen-check actual-source default-label probe fails four checks with the old helper, fails four different checks with the intermediate helper, and passes with the final correction. Physical provider binding and installed owner/UI acceptance remain separate open contracts.
+
+## Confirmed producer gap
+
+The actual diarization path assigns final speaker labels and commits transcript.json, then advances the pipeline. It did not refresh context.json. The live extractor and reconciler embed their source default (Others) in context items; those fields therefore disagree with the subsequently labelled transcript. The confirmed speaker-name sheet also saved Meeting.speakerNames without refreshing context. Notes regeneration already uses that existing map.
+
+Read-only analysis of the completed private Zoom files finds four possible changed attributions if only exact final words and source are used. Requiring a unique retained live quote plus its clock anchor leaves **three** supported changes: one commitment and two questions. The fourth exact quote is outside the original live interval. No owner file was edited; metadata only is in analysis.json. Different live/final ASR wording, ambiguous repeats and unavailable timing evidence remain unresolved.
+
+## Existing seam repaired
+
+- DiarizationService.commitSpeakerLabels is the actual final assignment handoff: require an existing meeting and successful transcript commit, preserve existing revision/retired-map behavior, then refresh the context. A failed transcript save cannot project uncommitted labels. A rejected context projection save throws through the existing diarization problem path while retaining the already saved transcript and previous context/current state. A known meeting without context is a successful no-op; an existing unreadable context is refused. The pre-existing retired-name metadata save still does not propagate its failure and remains open.
+- MeetingContextStore.refreshSpeakers reads the committed transcript and existing transcript.live.json once, applies the existing speakerNames map, and writes the existing context file only when attribution changed. No second store, queue, model pass, capture await or timer is added.
+- Ordinary context rows require the exact quote (case/whitespace normalization only), the same audio source, a unique retained live anchor, and an unambiguous final label containing that clock point. A repeated quote across overlapping final labels is refused. Short turns under twelve characters are left alone.
+- Candidate rows already retain evidenceStart: require it and exact same-source speech within that interval. No missing anchor is fabricated, and no fuzzy/LCS semantic match is used for identity.
+- Only speaker fields change. Source authority, action content, quote, recipient, confidence, IDs, clocks, participant list and task authorization remain unchanged. The participant list retains unknown labels rather than declaring an invented roster.
+- A confirmed speaker-name save refreshes context only after MeetingStore accepts the edited record. Default service dependencies remain lazy; injected isolated stores do not initialize the owner singleton.
+
+## Regression contract
+
+MeetingSpeakerProjectionSelfTest exercises the actual DiarizationService handoff, temporary MeetingStore transcript save, current context, and context file reload. It includes user-confirmed rename delivery, missing/foreign/deleted meetings, failed transcript/context saves, retained context bytes/current state, known no-context producer handoff, unreadable context refusal, source authority, ambiguous overlap/repeats, wrong source, absent live clock, later identical wording, absent candidate clock, agent-command exclusion, tiny turns, content equality and unchanged context bytes on repeated refresh. Root hooks this into the existing meeting-context flag. The unchanged original fixture should fail when its committed-label handoff omits context refresh; a passing helper alone is insufficient acceptance.
+
+Native optimized typechecking and the actual producer/consumer fixture pass in staged build SHA256 `9b17a28e668cdbaac94fab2a85c2c31daa97c812172c577cc5dda8b1564154c7`. [Native test log](../../2026-10-07-quality-loop/meeting-context-second-release.log) and [candidate pin](../../2026-10-07-quality-loop/second-candidate.json) record scope. This result does not establish installed owner UI or physical next-call naming acceptance.
+
+## Provider path and remaining acceptance
+
+Existing CallDetector emits no roster/conference URL for detected calls; MeetingScheduler.calendarMatch can enrich a scheduled call from its invitation. An invitation is not attendance or voice evidence. The prior [participant integration contract](../../2026-10-07-zoom-audit/PARTICIPANT-IDENTITY.md) remains the plan.
+
+Google Meet's authorized participants endpoint is available to meeting-space owners or participants and yields display names; signed-in IDs can interoperate with People API, while guests/phone users do not promise email. Its transcript entries include a participant reference and time interval, providing a stronger utterance binding when an accessible artifact exists. [Participant guide](https://developers.google.com/workspace/meet/api/guides/participants), [transcript entry reference](https://developers.google.com/workspace/meet/api/reference/rest/v2/conferenceRecords.transcripts.entries).
+
+Zoom's past participant endpoint requires a paid Pro-or-higher account and proper grants; user_email can be empty for people outside the host account. This is not proof that an arbitrary attendee's companion app can access a roster or bind voices. [Zoom Meetings API](https://developers.zoom.us/docs/api/meetings/#operation/pastMeetingParticipants).
+
+No provider grant, network identity lookup, browser/native meeting interaction or automatic name producer was implemented or run. The ended Zoom files contain no participant-linked artifact or observed active-speaker evidence. Next live Zoom/Meet observation must verify actual UI/API identity, timeline alignment, late joins, duplicate names, guests, rooms, overlap, insufficient grants and cancellation before universal naming can be promised. Re-clustering can change which voice a surviving label represents; preservation of an old dictionary key is not proof of identity continuity across such a pass.
+
+## Deleted-meeting native boundary restoration
+
+Review found an introduced lifecycle regression in the initial extraction: the new committed-label helper correctly returned without creating a missing meeting, but its Void return let native diarization continue into voice-print writes. The original `guard store.meeting(id: id) != nil else { return false }` is restored immediately before the helper call, with no intervening await. This exits before voice-print output, indexing resolution and successful diarization return. The isolated actual helper regression still proves no missing directory or revision change. The downstream native early-return control flow is source-reviewed restoration of the exact original guard; no model-backed delete-during-diarization observation is claimed.
+
+## Initial native fixture failure retained
+
+The first optimized meeting-context run failed exactly `content ids confidence clocks participants unchanged`; root reports the other 38 checks passed. That assertion compared decoded context to a pre-save in-memory object. The existing context writer uses ISO8601 dates without fractional seconds, so it already normalizes subsecond Date values independently of this repair. The initial fixture also added candidates in memory without persisting their original baseline.
+
+The corrected fixture calls the actual original ingest/save/load boundary before the label handoff, seeds one explicit fractional Date to expose that existing serialization behavior, and captures its real durable baseline. It separately checks exact in-memory payload equality and durable-to-durable equality after restoring only the two changed speaker fields. No production serializer, timestamp policy, input speech, speaker mapping or metadata invariant was relaxed. This is a fixture correction, not a product precision repair. The corrected fixture passes in the second optimized candidate; its marker is `MEETING_SPEAKER_PROJECTION_OK checks=51`. The initial failed assertion remains retained in the report.
+
+The next concrete platform work is broken into independently accepted slices in [NEXT-PARTICIPANTS.md](NEXT-PARTICIPANTS.md). The [default-label original/fixed fixture](default-label-regression/executed-metadata.json) retains actual helper/type pins, unchanged generic inputs and actual execution verdicts.
+
+## Explicit manual default-name compatibility
+
+Source review of the existing UI shows that nil/unlabelled system speech displays Others but is not offered in the rename sheet: `MeetingDiarizer.labels` lists only explicit segment.speaker values. A stored explicit Others value, however, is accepted by that function and offered by the same unfiltered sheet; its saved speakerNames mapping is consumed by transcript and notes display. The intermediate helper's unconditional default-label refusal would also reject that valid explicit mapping. No physical UI interaction or actual owner occurrence is claimed.
+
+A correction is staged outside package Sources while another agent compiles: a nonempty explicit speakerNames mapping wins, while fallback Others without such a map still supplies no identity. `manual-default-staged.patch` and metadata pin that preparation. The unchanged fourteen-check generic probe separates old no-map erasure, intermediate explicit-map rejection and the corrected intended behavior. The cached native fixture includes both the no-map refusal and actual saved-map consumer assertions. Root executed the unchanged fourteen-check probe: old helper FAIL4, intermediate helper FAIL4, corrected helper PASS14. [Executed metadata](default-label-regression/executed-metadata.json) preserves the actual results. The second optimized consumer passes51 with the correction; this does not prove physical UI interaction or any provider identity observation.
+
+A one-shot participant-surface diagnostic is staged in owned cache, with an existing bounded browser-read seam patch and an existing Zoom AX read wrapper. It is source-only: not compiled, integrated, invoked or accepted. See [diagnostic preparation metadata](participant-observation-staged-metadata.json). It returns raw private provenance, never guessed participant or active-speaker facts; the existing unbounded AX reader keeps Zoom use strictly isolated and diagnostic until its own bounded contract is repaired.
